    "content": "{\n  \"files\": [\n    {\n      \"content\": \"#include \\\"knn.h\\\"\\n\\n// Max-heap node structure\\ntypedef struct {\\n    float dist;\\n    char label;\\n} HeapNode;\\n\\nstatic inline void heap_swap(HeapNode *a, HeapNode *b) {\\n    HeapNode t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t;\\n}\\n\\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group * groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\\n    // Allocate labels array (initialized with -1 sentinel)\\n    char *labels = (char *) malloc(sizeof(char) * k);\\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) labels[i] = -1;\\n\\n    // Max-heap of size k+1 (index 1..k)\\n    HeapNode *heap = (HeapNode *) malloc(sizeof(HeapNode) * (k + 1));\\n    int heap_size = 0;\\n\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\\n        Group g = groups[i];\\n        for (int j = 0; j < g.length; j++) {\\n            float d = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate, g.points[j]);\\n\\n            if (heap_size < k) {\\n                // Insert into heap\\n                heap_size++;\\n                int idx = heap_size;\\n                heap[idx].dist = d;\\n                heap[idx].label = g.label;\\n                while (idx > 1 && heap[idx/2].dist < heap[idx].dist) {\\n                    heap_swap(&heap[idx], &heap[idx/2]);\\n                    idx /= 2;\\n                }\\n            } else if (d < heap[1].dist) {\\n                // Replace root\\n                heap[1].dist = d;\\n                heap[1].label = g.label;\\n                int idx = 1;\\n                while (1) {\\n                    int largest = idx;\\n                    int left = 2 * idx;\\n                    int right = 2 * idx + 1;\\n                    if (left <= heap_size && heap[left].dist > heap[largest].dist)\\n                        largest = left;\\n                    if (right <= heap_size && heap[right].dist > heap[largest].dist)\\n                        largest = right;\\n                    if (largest != idx) {\\n                        heap_swap(&heap[idx], &heap[largest]);\\n                        idx = largest;\\n                    } else break;\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // Copy labels from heap into labels array\\n    for (int i = 0; i < heap_size && i < k; i++) {\\n        labels[i] = heap[i+1].label;\\n    }\\n\\n    free(heap);\\n\\n    qsort(labels, k, sizeof(char), compare_for_sort);\\n\\n    char most_frequent = labels[0];\\n    int most_frequent_count = 1;\\n    int current_frequency = 1;\\n\\n    for (int i = 1; i < k; i++) {\\n        if (labels[i] != labels[i - 1]) {\\n            if (current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\\n                most_frequent = labels[i - 1];\\n                most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\\n            }\\n\\n            current_frequency = 1;\\n        } else {\\n            current_frequency++;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (i == k - 1 && current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\\n            most_frequent = labels[i - 1];\\n            most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    free(labels);\\n    return most_frequent;\\n}\\n\\nint main() {\\n    int n_groups = parse_number_of_groups();\\n    \\n    Group * groups = (Group *) malloc(sizeof(Group) * n_groups);\\n\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\\n        groups[i] = parse_next_group();\\n    }\\n\\n    int k = parse_k();\\n\\n    Point to_evaluate = parse_point();\\n\\n    printf(\\\"%c\\\", knn(n_groups, groups, k, to_evaluate));\\n}\\n\",\n      \"path\": \"src/knn.c\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"content\": \"#include <stdio.h>\\n#include <stdlib.h>\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    float x;\\n    float y;\\n} Point;\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    char label;\\n    int length;\\n    Point * points;\\n} Group;\\n\\nvoid on_error() {\\n    printf(\\\"Invalid input file.\\\\n\\\");\\n    exit(1);\\n}\\n\\nint parse_number_of_groups() {\\n    int n;\\n    if (scanf(\\\" n_groups=%d \\\", &n) != 1) on_error();\\n\\n    return n;\\n}\\n\\nPoint parse_point() {\\n    float x, y;\\n    if (scanf(\\\" (%f ,%f) \\\", &x, &y) != 2)  on_error();\\n\\n    Point point;\\n    point.x = x;\\n    point.y = y;\\n\\n    return point;\\n}\\n\\nGroup parse_next_group() {\\n    char label; \\n    int length;\\n\\n    if (scanf(\\\" label=%c \\\", &label) != 1) on_error();\\n    if (scanf(\\\" length=%d \\\", &length) != 1) on_error();\\n\\n    Group group;\\n    group.label = label;\\n    group.length = length;\\n    group.points = (Point *) malloc(sizeof(Point) * length);\\n\\n    for (int i = 0; i < length; i++) {\\n        group.points[i] = parse_point();\\n    }\\n\\n    return group;\\n}\\n\\nint parse_k() {\\n    int k;\\n    if (scanf(\\\" k=%d \\\", &k) != 1) on_error();\\n\\n    return k;\\n}\\n\\nstatic inline float euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(Point a, Point b) {\\n    return ((b.x - a.x) * (b.x - a.x)) + ((b.y - a.y) * (b.y - a.y));\\n}\\n\\nint compare_for_sort(const void *a, const void *b) {\\n  return *(char*)a - *(char*)b;\\n}\\n\",\n      \"path\": \"src/knn.h\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Replacing insertion-sort-based top-k selection with a max-heap removes loop-carried dependencies in the inner shift loop, enabling compiler auto-vectorization of distance computation and reducing complexity from O(N*k) to O(N log k).\",\n  \"notes\": \"The heap approach maintains the same set of k smallest distances, preserving correctness. The distance computation loop becomes independent per point (no data dependency across iterations), allowing compiler SIMD auto-vectorization. Memory usage is O(k).\",\n  \"sources_kept\": [],\n  \"transformations\": [\n    \"Replace inner shift-loop (O(k)) for maintaining sorted nearest neighbors with a max-heap (priority queue) of size k.\",\n    \"Change euclidean_distance_no_sqrt function to static inline to encourage inlining and vectorization.\"\n  ]\n}",
    "thinking": "We need to propose one bounded HPC source-code experiment. The goal is \"legal SIMD/vectorization and loop dependencies\". We have the current sources for knn.c and knn.h. The baseline time is ~2.09 seconds, correct.\n\nOur role B: we should propose transformations that enable vectorization or remove loop-carried dependencies. Looking at the code:\n\nIn knn function:\n- Outer loops over groups and points.\n- Inner loop (x) iterates k times to find insertion position for new distance d into sorted distances array. This inner loop has a dependency because it modifies distances and labels arrays, and there's a break after inserting. The structure is: for x from 0 to k-1, if d < distances[x] or distances[x]==-1, then shift elements from k-1 down to x+1 (y loop), insert at x, break.\n\nThis pattern prevents vectorization because of the conditional and data-dependent shifts. However, we can potentially replace this insertion sort with a simpler approach: maintain an unsorted list of k nearest neighbors? But the algorithm requires selecting k smallest distances. The current method maintains sorted order incrementally. Could use a max-heap (priority queue) to keep top-k smallest distances? That would be O(log k) per point instead of O(k). But that changes algorithm, might not vectorize either.\n\nAlternatively, we could separate distance computation from selection: compute all distances into an array, then sort or nth_element. But the number of points can be large (up to maybe 10^5?), and storing all distances may use too much memory? The problem statement doesn't give constraints but typical KNN for this contest might have moderate sizes.\n\nAnother approach: Use SIMD to compute multiple distances at once, then reduce. For each point in a group, we can compute distance using vectorized operations (e.g., SSE/AVX). But the current code computes one distance per iteration and inserts into sorted list. We could batch compute distances for several points, store them temporarily, then insert all? That might still have dependencies.\n\nBetter: Change the data structure to a fixed-size array of k elements that we keep unsorted but use selection algorithm (like quickselect) at the end? But we need to maintain top-k as we go. Could use a min-heap for largest distances? Actually, we want smallest distances. We can maintain a max-heap of size k: if heap size < k, push; else if new distance < heap.top(), pop and push. This is O(log k) per point, no inner loop over k. That removes the shift dependency and may allow vectorization of distance computation.\n\nBut we must ensure correctness. The current algorithm sorts distances ascending (smallest first). The insertion sort maintains sorted order. Using a max-heap for smallest distances: after processing all points, heap contains k smallest distances but not sorted; then we need to extract them in increasing order? Actually, the final step uses qsort on labels array which is built from the inserted order. In current code, labels are stored in same order as distances (sorted ascending). Then it counts frequencies of consecutive equal labels after sorting with qsort. If we use a heap, we would store pairs (distance, label) and at end extract k elements to get sorted order? But then we need to sort by distance to match original behavior? The final frequency counting uses the sorted labels array; but note that it sorts labels using qsort on char values, not distances. That means after insertion, labels are in same order as distances (sorted ascending). Then they sort labels alphabetically and count consecutive runs. So the ordering of labels by distance matters only for tie-breaking? Actually, if two points have equal distance, their labels could be in any order; but final classification depends on majority among k nearest neighbors regardless of internal order. The qsort sorts labels char values, so it groups same labels together irrespective of original distance order. So the heap approach should work: we just need to collect k smallest distances and their labels, then sort labels by character value (or count frequencies directly). We can store them in an array after extraction.\n\nThus transformation: Replace insertion-sort-based top-k with a max-heap (priority queue) of size k using binary heap. This removes the inner loop over x and y that has dependencies. Also allows vectorizing distance computation? Possibly we can compute distances for multiple points at once using SIMD, but need to be careful about memory layout.\n\nGiven constraints: 4 cores, ARM64 architecture, no GPU. Compiler likely GCC or ICC. We should aim for auto-vectorization by compiler with simple loops and no dependencies.\n\nWe could also restructure the distance computation loop to use OpenMP SIMD pragma? But we need legal vectorization; removing loop-carried dependency is key.\n\nProposed transformations:\n1. Replace insertion-sort top-k selection with a max-heap (priority queue) of size k.\n2. Use explicit array for heap storage and implement push/pop operations inline to avoid function call overhead.\n3. Possibly use OpenMP SIMD on distance computation loop? But the inner loop over points is independent per point; we can compute distances in parallel using SIMD if compiler vectorizes.\n\nBut note: The current code has nested loops: for each group, for each point in that group, compute distance and insert into heap. This is sequential across groups/points but insertion is O(log k). That's fine.\n\nWe also need to ensure the final frequency counting works correctly with labels from heap extraction (which will be unsorted by distance). We can extract all k elements from heap into arrays distances_out and labels_out, then sort labels using qsort as before. Or we could count frequencies directly without sorting: use a hash map? But simpler: after extracting, sort labels.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Define struct HeapNode { float dist; char label; }.\n- Use array of size k+1 for heap (1-indexed).\n- Functions: heap_push(HeapNode* heap, int *size, float d, char l) and maybe heap_pop if needed. But we only need to maintain top-k smallest distances using max-heap: when inserting new node, if heap size < k, push; else if d < heap[1].dist (root is largest), replace root and sift down.\n- At end, extract all nodes into arrays by repeatedly popping? Actually, after processing all points, the heap contains k smallest distances but not sorted. We need to get them in some order for frequency counting. Since we only care about labels, we can just iterate over heap array (size k) and collect labels, then sort with qsort. The distance ordering doesn't matter because final step sorts by label char value anyway. So we don't need to extract sorted; we can directly copy the heap nodes into arrays of size k (they are in arbitrary order). Then sort labels.\n\nBut careful: In current code, distances array is used only for insertion comparison; after that it's not used. Labels array is built and then sorted by char value. So we don't need to preserve distance ordering at all. The heap approach will give us the correct set of k smallest distances (and their labels). Then we can just copy labels into an array, sort with qsort, count frequencies.\n\nThus transformation simplifies code and removes inner loop dependency.\n\nPotential issue: The current insertion method uses -1 as sentinel for uninitialized slots. With heap, we initialize size=0.\n\nAlso need to handle case where k is 0? Not likely but safe.\n\nNow, regarding SIMD/vectorization: After removing the data-dependent shift loop, the distance computation can be vectorized by compiler if loops are simple and no aliasing issues. We could also add restrict keywords or use aligned memory for points arrays. But we must not change tests; only source files under src/. So we can modify knn.c and knn.h.\n\nWe should keep other functions unchanged (parsing, etc). Only modify knn function in knn.c and possibly add helper functions in knn.h? Better to implement heap inline in knn.c to avoid adding new functions that might affect compilation units. But we can define static inline functions in knn.c or just write code directly.\n\nGiven the goal is \"legal SIMD/vectorization and loop dependencies\", this transformation removes a major dependency (the inner shift loop) which prevents vectorization of outer loops? Actually, the outer loops over groups and points are independent per point; but the insertion step creates a data dependency across iterations because it modifies distances array. With heap, each iteration only touches heap with O(log k) operations that have dependencies within themselves but not across different points (since heap state is updated). However, compiler may still not vectorize due to function calls or indirect indexing. But we can try.\n\nAlternative: Use a simpler approach: compute all distances into an array of size total_points? That would require storing all distances and labels, then using nth_element or sort to find top-k. This might be more amenable to SIMD because distance computation loop is pure and vectorizable. Then selection can be done with standard library (qsort on pairs). But memory usage could be large if many points; but problem constraints unknown.\n\nGiven the baseline time ~2s, likely input size moderate. We'll go with heap approach as it's bounded O(N log k) and removes inner loop dependency.\n\nWe also need to ensure correctness: compare output bytes exactly against reference. The algorithm must produce same classification for any valid input. Since we are selecting top-k smallest distances (same set), the final majority label should be identical regardless of internal ordering, provided ties are broken consistently? In current code, if there is a tie in distance among points with different labels, which one gets inserted first affects order but after sorting by char value and counting frequencies, does it affect result? The class